ecision made by  Chancellor Olaf Schultz  well for more let's talk to Tim Ripley  he's a defense journalist and writer and  joins us from Lancaster in England hi  and welcome to DW now what weapons and  equipment would you say Ukraine needs  the most right now  well you've got to decide what the  Ukrainian War aims are are there war  aims to defend their cities and and uh  protect their their territory that they  hold now or is there a ambition to  actually launch an offensive and drive  the Russians back from all the territory  occupy from the donbass and retake  Crimea those are two different types of  objectives and they require two  different types of weapons at the moment  the Western allies NATO U.S UK have have  provided them the equipment to do the  first thing they have not offered up  heavy weapons tanks fighter planes  attack helicopters so the ukrainians  could take the offensive and drive an  attempt to drive the Russians from the  donbass and from Crimea that would be a  major escalation of the war  well Tim we have been hearing about the  the looming Russian assault in Ukraine's  east and south how do you see it panning  out  well this is a a an area where the  Russian advantages in tanks and  artillery will will give them a a an  edge over the ukrainians in a way that  they didn't when they were fighting  around Kiev in in the cities and in in  the in the towns this is open terrain it  was famously fought over by the the  German Army in World War II and the  Soviet Army Battle of cursed the biggest  tank battle of World War II was fought  in this area so it plays to the  Russell's strength they can maneuver  they can try and Surround what's left of  the Ukrainian Army and do what they've  done in marry a poll to other Ukrainian  uh units in that area and and and they  are trying to inflict a defeat on the  Ukrainian Army to force them to agree to  terms or allow the Russians to claim  victory and open the way for some sort  of negotiation face-saving